{
  "id": "COMPOUND:689 - PROTEIN:813",
  "verbalization": "Naltrexone is implicated in the relationship with the Kappa-type opioid receptor through a multi-step mechanism involving the 5-hydroxytryptamine receptor 2C. This pathway is supported by three distinct but overlapping evidence chains, all of which point to Naltrexone as a target of the 5-hydroxytryptamine receptor 2C, which in turn targets the Kappa-type opioid receptor. The shared target of Naltrexone and the Kappa-type opioid receptor suggests a potential role in modulating opioid signaling, though the exact functional significance remains to be elucidated. The presence of multiple targets in the pathway, including a synthetic compound (3-Methylfentanyl) and a natural compound (Ginkgolide A), highlights the complexity of the interaction network. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of the 5-hydroxytryptamine receptor 2C as a central mediator in the relationship between Naltrexone and the Kappa-type opioid receptor.",
